The first thing I’d watch is the demand/supply mismatch showing up in gross margin, inventory, and receivables. NVDA says its gross margin was [10-K Item 7] (71.1% in fiscal 2026, down from 75.0%), and it says inventory provisions and excess purchase commitments already had an unfavorable 2.6% impact on gross margin in fiscal 2026. It also says the complexity of product transitions can cause delays, revenue volatility, inventory provisions, lower yields, higher material costs, and higher warranty costs. That is the earliest line where several of the core bear risks would surface. [[10-K Item 7], [10-K Item 7]]
What I’d watch in the reported numbers:
- gross margin: [10-K Item 7], because the filing says inventory provisions and mix/transition issues hit it first;
- inventory: $31.57B (2026-07-26), because excess inventory is the direct balance-sheet consequence of demand falling behind supply;
- accounts receivable: $63.06B (2026-07-26), because if customers slow purchases or timing stretches, receivables tend to move with it;
- capex: $6.04B (2026-01-25), but this is more a capacity / investment signal than the first warning line.

The reason this ranks first is that NVDA itself says it is using long lead times, advance purchases, deposits, and non-cancellable commitments to secure supply, so the first reported stress tends to be in the gross margin and working-capital lines, not in revenue alone. It explicitly says demand changes, product transitions, and customer deferrals can create excess or obsolete inventory and excess purchase commitments. [[10-K Item 7]]
The next risk after that is the China/export-control line. The filing says the US government required a license for H20 exports, and that resulted in a charge for excess inventory and purchase obligations; it also says they have not yet generated revenue under the H200 program. That one can show up quickly, but in the reported numbers it tends to arrive through charges, gross margin, and revenue mix rather than as a separate line item by itself. [[10-K Item 7]]
So, if you want the single “earliest” tell, it’s gross margin first, then inventory. If you want the exact watchlist, it is gross margin, inventory, receivables, product-transition charges or inventory provisions, and revenue mix in Data Center and China-related commentary.

What would change the reading:
- if gross margin holds up or expands while revenue keeps rising, the demand/supply bear case is weaker;
- if inventory keeps rising faster than revenue, the bear case strengthens;
- if receivables rise out of proportion to revenue, that points to timing pressure or collection stretch;
- if export-control charges recur or China revenue stays minimal, the regulatory bear case strengthens.

